===Etymology===
{{etymon|en|:from|risque<id:risk>|tree=1}}
From earlier {{m|en|risque}}, from {{bor|en|frm|risque}}, from {{der|en|roa-oit|risco|t=risk}} (modern {{cog|it|rischio}}) and {{m|it|rischiare|t=to run into danger}}. Displaced native {{cog|ang|pleoh||risk}} and {{m|ang|plēon||to risk}}.
{{col-top|2|Speculation on earlier roots}}
Most dictionaries consider the etymology of these Italian terms {{unc|en|nocap=1}}, but some suggest they perhaps come from {{der|en|la-vul|*resecum||that which cuts, rock, crag}} (> {{cog|la-med|resicu}}), from {{der|en|la|resecō||cut off, loose, curtail|pos=verb}}, in the sense of that which is a danger to boating or shipping; or from {{der|en|grc|ῥιζικόν||root, radical, hazard}}.

A few dictionaries express more certainty. Collins says the {{der|en|it|risco}} comes from {{der|en|grc|ῥίζα||cliff}} due to the hazards of sailing along rocky coasts. The American Heritage says it probably comes from {{der|en|gkm|ῥιζικό|ῥιζικό, ριζικό|t=sustenance obtained by a soldier through his own initiative, fortune}}, from {{der|en|ar|رِزْق|t=sustenance, that which God allots}}, from {{der|en|syc|ܪܙܩܐ|ܪܘܙܝܩܐ ,ܪܙܩܐ|tr=rezqā, rōzīqā|t=daily ration}}, from {{der|en|pal|tr=rōzīg}} (whence {{cog|fa-cls|روزِی}}), from {{der|en|pal|tr=rōz|t=day}} (whence {{cog|fa-cls|روز}}), from {{der|en|peo|𐎼𐎢𐎨𐏃|tr=raučah}} (cognate with {{cog|sa|रोचस्|t=[[light]], [[lustre]]}}), from {{der|en|ira-pro|*ráwčah}}, from {{der|en|iir-pro|*ráwčas}}, ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*léwkos|*léwk-os}}, from {{suf|ine-pro|*lewk-|t1=bright; to shine|*-os|pos2=noun suffix|nocat=1}}. Karla Mallette derives the word from {{der|en|ar|رِزْق|t=sustenance, that which God allots}} via {{bor|en|pml|-}}.<ref>{{cite-web |title=Mediterranean lingua franca, ca. 1450-1650: Threshold or holdover? |url=https://studyingteachingthemediterranean.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2018/07/karla-mallette-lingua-franca.pdf |accessdate=4 September 2025 |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250813071833/https://studyingteachingthemediterranean.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2018/07/karla-mallette-lingua-franca.pdf|archivedate=13 August 2025}}</ref>

